  <preface xml:id="preface-about">
    <title>About the Math Trailhead</title>


    <p>
      The <em>Math Trailhead</em> is a self-paced review of the algebra,
      functions, and trigonometry that Reed's calculus sequence assumes.
      It is organized by topic rather than by course, so you can start
      wherever you feel shaky and work outward from there.
    </p>


    <p>
      If you would like a suggestion for where to begin: headed for
      <em>MATH 111</em>, start with <xref ref="functions" text="title"/> and
      <xref ref="algebra" text="title"/>, then work through whichever of the
      other chapters feels least familiar. Headed for <em>MATH 112</em>, make
      sure you are quick with <xref ref="inequalities" text="title"/>,
      especially absolute values, alongside
      <xref ref="functions" text="title"/>. Headed for <em>STAT 141</em>,
      start with <xref ref="quantitative_reasoning" text="title"/>, then
      <xref ref="algebra" text="title"/>. Headed for <em>MATH 113</em>,
      start with <xref ref="algebra" text="title"/> and the counting section
      of <xref ref="quantitative_reasoning" text="title"/>.
    </p>


    <p>
      Every exercise here is a live <term>WeBWorK</term> problem. Enter an
      answer and submit it for immediate feedback, and use the option to
      generate a new version of a problem when you want more practice with
      the same idea. Nothing you type is recorded or reported to anyone:
      this is a place to practice, not an assignment.
    </p>


    <p>
      Corrections and suggestions are welcome. If a problem renders
      strangely, or a correct answer is marked wrong, please contact
      Kyle Ormsby at <email>ormsbyk@reed.edu</email>. There is also a
      <q>Feedback</q> button on every page.
    </p>
  </preface>


  <acknowledgement xml:id="acknowledgements">
    <title>Acknowledgments</title>


    <p>
      <em>Math Trailhead</em> owes its existence to Caleb Fishman Miller
      and Aisha Mandhro, who developed its initial version over a summer
      at Reed: gathering and organizing several hundred WeBWorK problems
      and building them into the PreTeXt source this site is generated
      from. Their work is the foundation of everything here.
    </p>


    <p>
      Nearly every exercise in this book is drawn from the
      <url href="https://github.com/openwebwork/webwork-open-problem-library">WeBWorK
      Open Problem Library</url>, a freely licensed collection of tens of
      thousands of problems assembled over two decades. Our thanks to the
      people who created it, to those who maintain it, and to the many
      instructors who have contributed problems to it. A resource of this
      size was a summer's work only because theirs came first.
    </p>
  </acknowledgement>


